d9bc11fd-5a4e-4889-9960-fb512a7d4ca6	0cf1c7c9-f953-4bbd-83eb-894416c2b43c	This paper addresses the limitation of short context windows in classic encoder-only language models, which are insufficient for processing long documents. The research aims to develop a high-quality Polish language model capable of handling significantly longer sequences than existing models.	The researchers introduced a Polish model with a context window of up to 8192 tokens, trained using a two-stage procedure involving positional embedding adaptation and continuous pre-training. Compressed variants were developed through knowledge distillation. The models were evaluated on 25 tasks, including the KLEJ benchmark, a new financial task suite (FinBench), and other classification/regression tasks, particularly those requiring long-document understanding.	The proposed model achieved the best average performance among Polish and multilingual models on the evaluated tasks. It significantly outperformed competitive solutions in long-context tasks while maintaining comparable quality on short texts.	Not specified in abstract	This work advances the field of Polish language understanding by providing an efficient encoder-only model capable of processing long documents. It enables more effective analysis of lengthy texts for various discriminative tasks, offering a cost-effective alternative to decoder-only LLMs for such applications.	gemini-2.5-flash-lite	ready	\N	0	2026-03-14 00:14:26.403717+05:30	The abstract does not detail the specific performance metrics or scores achieved on individual tasks or benchmarks, making it difficult to quantify the exact improvement over baselines.\n\nThe abstract mentions compressed model variants but does not specify their performance relative to the full model or their efficiency gains (e.g., parameter count, inference speed).\n\nThe paper focuses on Polish language understanding. The effectiveness of the proposed long-context encoder architecture and training methodology on other low-resource languages is not explored.
dbebece2-fa6f-408a-8378-bb24823a5835	a02247ea-9db3-46bd-8e2f-dc7beee4643f	This paper addresses the challenge of data heterogeneity in federated learning (FL). Existing FL methods like FedAvg, IFCA, and Ditto struggle with client differences, especially under sparse or multi-dimensional heterogeneity, motivating the need for a more robust approach.	The proposed method conditions a single global model on locally-computed PCA statistics of each client's training data, requiring no additional communication. Experiments were conducted across 97 configurations, including four heterogeneity types (label shift, covariate shift, concept shift, combined), four datasets (MNIST, Fashion-MNIST, CIFAR-10, CIFAR-100), and seven FL baseline methods.	The proposed method matches the Oracle baseline across all tested settings. It surpasses the Oracle baseline by 1-6% on combined heterogeneity, outperforming it where continuous statistics are richer than discrete cluster identifiers. The method is also uniquely robust to sparsity compared to all other tested methods.	Not specified in abstract	This work advances federated learning by providing a communication-efficient and robust method for handling data heterogeneity. It enables better performance in real-world FL scenarios where client data distributions vary significantly and can be sparse.	gemini-2.5-flash-lite	ready	\N	0	2026-03-13 22:58:10.564843+05:30	The abstract does not specify the computational overhead of computing PCA statistics locally on clients. Further research could quantify this overhead across different client hardware capabilities and dataset sizes.\n\nThe paper focuses on PCA statistics. It's unclear how other dimensionality reduction techniques or feature extraction methods would perform when used for client conditioning.\n\nThe evaluation covers four types of heterogeneity, but the interaction and impact of combining more than two heterogeneity types simultaneously are not deeply explored.
49a9074a-16a0-4a57-9596-17f90b653949	2bbcba79-303c-4401-b407-0607c4cf0c50	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	failed	2026-03-13 23:28:27.760348+05:30	1	2026-03-13 23:28:27.762364+05:30	\N
10df62e1-a685-4776-b9d6-6aceed5aa842	68d40ae5-7e7e-467d-9656-131d443d9d48	This paper addresses the limitation of current Transcription Factor (TF) binding site prediction methods that primarily focus on individual TFs and binary classification. It aims to investigate TF binding site recognition as a multi-label classification problem to capture interactions and cooperative regulatory mechanisms among various TFs.	The study employs deep learning models based on Temporal Convolutional Networks (TCNs) for multi-label classification of TF binding sites. These models are designed to predict multiple TF binding profiles simultaneously, thereby capturing correlations among TFs and their cooperative regulatory mechanisms. The models were trained and evaluated on DNA sequences retrieved from public repositories.	The proposed multi-label learning framework achieves reliable predictive performances for multiple TFs on DNA sequences. The results suggest that the model can reveal biologically meaningful motifs and co-binding patterns consistent with known TF interactions. Furthermore, the framework also suggests novel relationships and cooperation among TFs.	Not specified in abstract	This research advances the field of TF binding site prediction by introducing a multi-label classification approach using TCNs. It enables a more comprehensive understanding of TF binding logic, including their interactions and cooperative mechanisms, moving beyond single-TF binary predictions.	gemini-2.5-flash-lite	ready	\N	0	2026-03-13 23:28:52.938074+05:30	The abstract does not specify the exact performance metrics (e.g., precision, recall, F1-score) or the specific datasets used for training and evaluation. A future research paper could focus on a rigorous quantitative evaluation of the TCN framework across various benchmark datasets for TF binding prediction, comparing its performance against existing state-of-the-art methods using standardized metrics.\n\nThe abstract mentions 'suggesting novel relationships and cooperation among TFs' but does not detail how these novel findings were validated or explored further. A follow-up study could investigate the biological validation of these suggested novel TF interactions, potentially using experimental data or integrating other biological information sources.\n\nThe current framework is based on TCNs. It is unclear how other deep learning architectures, such as Transformers or Graph Neural Networks, might perform for this multi-label temporal binding site characterization task. A comparative study exploring different deep learning architectures could reveal alternative or complementary approaches.
8831c476-9512-4dd4-a5da-bb51c40a0d98	23e528ea-89f8-4983-b6e6-cd58cb642567	This paper addresses the difficulty in achieving fine-grained control over generated images in text-to-image models, stemming from a limited understanding of how semantic information is encoded. Specifically, it aims to interpret the color representation within the latent space of the FLUX.1 Variational Autoencoder.	The researchers developed an interpretation of the color representation in the Variational Autoencoder latent space of FLUX.1, termed the Latent Color Subspace (LCS). This interpretation was verified by demonstrating its ability to predict and control color. The method involves training-free, closed-form latent-space manipulation.	The study reveals a structure within the FLUX.1 latent space that reflects Hue, Saturation, and Lightness. The proposed Latent Color Subspace (LCS) interpretation successfully predicts and explicitly controls color in generated images. This is achieved through a fully training-free method.	Not specified in abstract	This work advances the understanding of latent space representations in text-to-image models by revealing an emergent order related to color. It provides a practical, training-free method for explicit color control, which can significantly enhance the fine-grained controllability of generative models.	gemini-2.5-flash-lite	ready	\N	0	2026-03-13 23:31:51.953787+05:30	The abstract does not specify the performance metrics used to evaluate the prediction and control capabilities of the LCS. A potential research paper could involve a quantitative evaluation of LCS's color prediction accuracy and control fidelity using standard color difference metrics (e.g., Delta E) across diverse image datasets.\n\nThe current LCS interpretation is demonstrated on FLUX.1. It is unclear if this latent color structure is a general property of other Variational Autoencoders or text-to-image models. A new project could investigate the presence and nature of LCS in other state-of-the-art generative models like Stable Diffusion or Imagen.\n\nThe abstract focuses on color control. It does not explore whether other semantic attributes, beyond color, also exhibit emergent, controllable subspaces within the FLUX.1 latent space. A research paper could explore the existence and manipulation of latent subspaces for other semantic features like texture, style, or object properties.
