d265009b-7f67-4127-bd1b-7c8b0a59184f	dbe07ad3-5a3b-42ac-a04c-a14a7d213229	This study aimed to develop and validate a machine learning model to predict 30-day mortality in cardiac surgery patients. It also sought to create a clinician-oriented web application for real-time use of this predictive model.	A retrospective cohort of 325 cardiac surgery patients was analyzed using supervised machine learning. After data preprocessing and feature selection, the XGBoost model was trained and evaluated via cross-validation, achieving the best performance. A web application was developed using StreamLit, incorporating SHAP for model explainability.	The XGBoost model demonstrated strong performance with an AUC-ROC of 0.968, a recall of 0.800, and a Brier score of 0.058. Preliminary clinical feedback suggested the web application was intuitive, informative, and potentially useful for preoperative risk assessment.	The study analyzed a retrospective cohort of 325 patients, which may limit generalizability. The abstract does not explicitly state other limitations acknowledged by the authors.	This paper offers a practical tool by integrating a robust machine learning model with a functional clinical application. This combination enhances both the accuracy and accessibility of 30-day mortality prediction, supporting clinical decision-making in cardiac surgery.	gemini-2.5-flash-lite	ready	\N	0	2026-03-14 20:02:01.118568+05:30	Multicentre validation of the developed model and application.\n\nUser-centered refinement of the web application based on broader clinical feedback.\n\nComparison of the ML model's predictive accuracy against existing clinical risk scores.
