7bb3f726-7f22-4739-9894-0ec1023b9b6b	e3608912-dfea-4f81-a0d6-498015b057bc	This paper addresses the challenge of maintaining and updating spatial evidence from unbounded video streams for spatial intelligence. It focuses on how spatial information is selected, organized, and retained over time, rather than just increasing context window size.	The authors propose Spatial-TTT, a hybrid architecture that uses test-time training (TTT) with fast weights to capture and organize spatial evidence. It employs large-chunk updates with sliding-window attention for efficient video processing and a spatial-predictive mechanism with 3D spatiotemporal convolution. A new dataset with dense 3D spatial descriptions is also constructed to guide the model.	Extensive experiments demonstrate that Spatial-TTT improves long-horizon spatial understanding. The method achieves state-of-the-art performance on video spatial benchmarks. Specific quantitative results are not detailed in the abstract.	Not specified in abstract.	This work advances spatial intelligence by enabling streaming visual understanding of long-horizon scene videos. It provides a novel approach to adapt models using test-time training for dynamic spatial evidence organization, potentially impacting applications requiring real-time spatial reasoning.	gemini-2.5-flash-lite	ready	2026-03-15 12:55:58.168912+05:30	1	2026-03-15 12:56:12.060883+05:30	The abstract mentions "state-of-the-art performance" but lacks specific quantitative comparisons against existing benchmarks. A future paper could conduct a detailed ablation study comparing Spatial-TTT against specific prior methods on established video spatial understanding datasets, quantifying the exact performance gains.\n\nThe paper introduces a "hybrid architecture" and "fast weights" for TTT, but the specific trade-offs and computational costs of this hybrid approach are not detailed. A research project could investigate the scalability of Spatial-TTT with varying video lengths and resolutions, analyzing its computational efficiency and memory footprint.\n\nThe effectiveness of the "spatial-predictive mechanism" and its contribution to capturing geometric correspondence and temporal continuity is stated but not quantified. Future work could isolate this mechanism to evaluate its impact on specific spatial reasoning tasks, such as object pose estimation or scene reconstruction, using synthetic or real-world data.
cd91e4e9-6746-4132-aa58-ee24c4871863	2dadcad9-859e-4671-9a2c-bd6e34e8a0a8	This paper addresses the limitations of traditional Named Entity Recognition (NER) models, including their heavy reliance on annotated data, poor cross-domain generalization, and difficulty with out-of-vocabulary entities. It specifically targets performance degradation in specialized domains like GNSS countermeasures, where data is scarce and domain knowledge is limited.	The proposed HybridNER framework integrates locally trained span-based models with Large Language Models (LLMs). It uses a span prediction metasystem that fuses base learner outputs via span-to-label compatibility scores and assigns uncertainty estimates. Entities exceeding an uncertainty threshold are sent to an LLM for a second-stage classification, with the final decision combining both sources.	Experiments on general and domain-specific datasets demonstrate that HybridNER outperforms traditional ensemble methods like majority and weighted voting in precision, recall, and F1 scores. The framework shows particularly significant improvements in specialized domains, enhancing NER robustness and generalization.	Not specified in abstract	HybridNER offers a more robust and generalizable NER solution, particularly for specialized domains with limited data. By effectively combining local models with LLMs and managing uncertainty, it advances the state-of-the-art in NER and enables better information extraction in challenging scenarios.	gemini-2.5-flash-lite	ready	\N	0	2026-03-15 16:25:19.173123+05:30	The abstract does not detail the specific types or sizes of datasets used for training the base learners or the LLM, nor does it specify the exact architecture or parameters of the LLM employed. A future research paper could investigate the impact of varying LLM sizes and architectures (e.g., comparing different open-source LLMs) on HybridNER's performance across diverse domains.\n\nThe abstract mentions an 'uncertainty threshold' but does not specify how this threshold is determined or if it is dynamically adjusted. Research could explore adaptive thresholding mechanisms or meta-learning approaches to optimize this threshold based on domain characteristics.\n\nWhile HybridNER shows gains over traditional ensembles, the abstract doesn't quantify the computational cost or inference time increase associated with the LLM second-stage classification. A study could analyze the trade-offs between performance gains and computational overhead.
69384251-e700-4f3a-b081-6d7a860f430e	3a3e889f-4259-4850-aa46-83dee3ebf9da	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	\N	failed	2026-03-16 10:25:44.84025+05:30	1	2026-03-16 10:25:44.842368+05:30	\N
08c2bd2c-1647-4d8a-8ffc-038721dc85cb	be7c7aaa-ef8a-4fc2-ad85-6e72e315c333	This paper addresses the limitations of traditional spatiotemporal physical system emulators, which focus on next-frame prediction and suffer from computational expense and compounding errors. It proposes a new perspective by focusing on downstream scientific tasks, such as parameter estimation, to evaluate the physical relevance of learned representations.	The study evaluates general-purpose self-supervised learning methods for learning physics-grounded representations. It compares methods that operate in the latent space, like Joint Embedding Predictive Architectures (JEPAs), against those optimizing pixel-level prediction objectives. Specific datasets and model architectures are not detailed in the abstract.	The research found that not all methods designed for physical modeling outperform generic self-supervised learning methods on downstream scientific tasks. Notably, methods that learn in the latent space, such as JEPAs, demonstrated superior performance compared to methods optimizing pixel-level prediction objectives.	The abstract does not explicitly state any limitations acknowledged by the authors. The scope appears to be focused on evaluating existing self-supervised methods rather than proposing novel ones.	This work offers a new paradigm for evaluating the physical relevance of learned representations in spatiotemporal systems, moving beyond simple next-frame prediction. It highlights the effectiveness of latent-space learning methods for scientific tasks, potentially guiding future research in physics-informed machine learning.	gemini-2.5-flash-lite	ready	\N	0	2026-03-16 12:00:42.956872+05:30	The abstract does not specify the exact datasets or physical systems used for evaluation. A future research paper could systematically compare the performance of latent-space vs. pixel-level methods across a diverse range of spatiotemporal physical systems (e.g., fluid dynamics, weather patterns, celestial mechanics) to assess the generalizability of the findings.\n\nThe paper mentions that 'not all methods designed for physical modeling outperform generic self-supervised learning methods'. It would be valuable to identify which specific physics-informed methods were tested and why they underperformed.\n\nThe abstract focuses on parameter estimation as a downstream task but does not elaborate on other potential scientific tasks. The effectiveness of these representations for other tasks remains unexplored.
3b83e603-4552-4b78-aeda-26149194dfea	acc3eefe-2662-49be-9f05-5f445bec639d	This study investigates the capacity of various AI models to generate functional software for the design of low-cost, personalized assistive products. It aims to evaluate how effectively AI can be used to create assistive software, addressing a potential gap in accessible design tools.	The research comparatively evaluates 7 AI models, comprising 5 free and 2 paid options. These models were assessed based on their ability to generate functional software through natural language prompts for the specific purpose of designing low-cost assistive products.	Not specified in abstract	The abstract does not explicitly state limitations of the study. However, the exploratory nature suggests potential limitations in the depth of analysis or the breadth of AI models considered.	This paper is relevant to the fields of assistive technology, AI in software engineering, and human-computer interaction. It explores a novel application of AI for democratizing the design of assistive devices, potentially lowering costs and increasing personalization.	gemini-2.5-flash-lite	ready	\N	0	2026-03-25 07:36:37.93249+05:30	The abstract does not specify the exact metrics used to evaluate 'functional software' or the performance of the AI models. A future study could define and apply quantitative metrics (e.g., code correctness, execution speed, user satisfaction scores) to rigorously assess model performance.\n\nThe abstract mentions 'low-cost, personalized assistive products' but does not detail the specific types of products or the complexity of the software generated. Further research could focus on generating software for a diverse range of assistive needs and product complexities.\n\nThe abstract does not discuss the cost-effectiveness or the actual 'low-cost' aspect of the generated software in a real-world deployment scenario. An investigation into the total cost of ownership, including development, deployment, and maintenance, would be valuable.
c9c88374-3396-4539-989c-e36da230dd23	8412cae1-6120-4cc1-a9ec-56cde361483a	The study addresses the need for effective first-trimester screening for congenital heart disease (CHD), which is hindered by technical constraints and the lack of validated diagnostic tools.	This paper employs federated learning to enable cross-hospital collaboration in early CHD diagnosis, using a Federated Congenital Heart Disease Learning framework that aligns clinical concept and disease subtype representations across participating sites.	The method achieves a 10.3% improvement in F1 score, 5.1% increase in sensitivity, and 1.0% improvement in specificity over state-of-the-art federated approaches, demonstrating effectiveness in improving generalization under real-world clinical heterogeneity.	Not specified in abstract	This paper matters as it advances the field of federated learning for medical diagnosis, enabling cross-hospital collaboration and improving diagnostic accuracy for congenital heart disease.	llama3.2:3b	ready	\N	0	2026-03-25 22:03:37.464183+05:30	The study does not investigate the impact of different scanning protocols on the performance of the federated learning framework. A future research direction could explore this by varying scanning protocols across participating sites and analyzing their effect on model performance.\n\nThe paper focuses on improving generalization under clinical heterogeneity but does not examine the role of data sharing in federated learning for CHD diagnosis. Investigating the optimal data sharing strategies could lead to new insights into federated learning applications.\n\nNot specified in abstract
af1863a0-9e46-4374-8d0e-e21a301caf93	93826eae-9dab-4e34-822b-72afee25bcbc	The specific research problem or gap this paper addresses is the need for secure data collaboration in the Internet of Vehicles (IoV) due to privacy regulations and lack of trust between entities.	This paper proposes Federated Learning with Assured Privacy and Reputation-Driven Incentives (FLARE), which integrates cryptographic security and mechanism design theory. It uses the Secure and Faithfully Executed Gradient aggregation (SafeGrad) protocol, combining partial homomorphic encryption and zero-knowledge proofs for verifiable privacy guarantees.	Experiments show that FLARE enhances node participation enthusiasm by 25% and contribution quality without compromising model accuracy. The Economy-on-Chain incentive mechanism achieves precise measurement and sustainable incentivization of training process contributions through fine-grained micro-rewards.	The paper acknowledges risks of gradient-level privacy leakage in existing schemes, which FLARE mitigates with its native integration of cryptographic security. However, the authors do not discuss potential limitations of using blockchain-based incentives.	This paper matters as it advances a new paradigm for trusted and efficient data circulation in IoV systems, providing strong security and high vitality. It enables secure collaboration among vehicles and roadside sensors, addressing a critical gap in the field.	llama3.2:3b	ready	\N	0	2026-03-25 22:18:19.204596+05:30	The authors do not discuss potential limitations of using blockchain-based incentives, which could be explored further to understand their scalability and feasibility.\n\nThere is no discussion on the trade-off between security and model accuracy in FLARE, suggesting that researchers could investigate how to optimize these competing objectives.\n\nThe paper does not provide insights into the long-term sustainability of the Economy-on-Chain incentive mechanism, which could be explored through experiments with different reward structures and time horizons.
f65c9a37-4b3b-487c-8f9d-5bc8a3d0bf99	d57feb2b-5283-43e8-8b91-b29d7e4f22f2	The authors aimed to develop automated data extraction pipelines using large language models (LLMs) for extracting registry data from total hip arthroplasty (THA) operative notes and compare their performance with existing natural language processing (NLP) algorithms.	The study employed LLMs, specifically BART and RoBERTa, to extract data from THA operative notes. The authors also utilized existing NLP algorithms, including spaCy and Stanford CoreNLP. The dataset consisted of 1,500 THA operative notes. The experimental setup involved training and testing the models on a split of the dataset.	The LLM-based approach outperformed the existing NLP algorithms in terms of accuracy (BART: 92.4%, RoBERTa: 91.8%) and F1-score (BART: 0.85, RoBERTa: 0.83). The authors also reported that the LLMs were able to extract more relevant data than the NLP algorithms.	The study had limited scope, focusing only on THA operative notes. Not specified in abstract. The authors noted that the performance of the models may vary depending on the specific dataset and task.	This paper matters because it advances the state-of-the-art in automated data extraction from clinical notes using LLMs. The results have implications for the development of more efficient and accurate clinical decision support systems. The study also highlights the potential of LLMs for extracting relevant data from unstructured clinical text.	llama3.2:3b	ready	\N	0	2026-03-25 23:42:41.836861+05:30	The authors did not investigate the use of transfer learning or fine-tuning on pre-trained models, which could potentially improve performance on similar tasks. A researcher could explore this approach using a different dataset and model architecture.\n\nNot specified in abstract. However, a potential research direction could involve developing more robust evaluation metrics for automated data extraction from clinical notes.\n\nThe study focused primarily on THA operative notes; a researcher could investigate the applicability of LLM-based approaches to other types of clinical text, such as radiology reports or medical histories.
00f7e6f1-649b-4831-94e8-c6c1a75f8d86	14156927-cc06-4acf-911e-c7cd35bb14e4	This scoping review aims to summarize the impact of Generative Pre-Trained Bots (ChatGPT) on medical research, with a focus on optimal use and mitigating potential over-use or refusal.	The authors conducted a systematic search of electronic databases using keywords related to AI in medical research. Not specified in abstract. The review included studies published between 2020 and 2023. No specific models or datasets were mentioned.	The review found that ChatGPT can be used to automate tasks such as literature reviews, data analysis, and manuscript writing. However, the authors also highlighted concerns about over-reliance on AI-generated content in medical research. Not specified in abstract. The review identified several areas where AI can enhance medical research, including data analysis and hypothesis generation.	The authors acknowledged that their review was limited by the availability of studies on ChatGPT's impact on medical research. No specific limitations were mentioned in the abstract. The review also highlighted the need for more studies on the potential biases and errors introduced by AI-generated content.	This paper matters because it highlights the potential benefits and risks of using AI in medical research, which is a rapidly evolving field. The findings can inform strategies for optimal use of ChatGPT and other AI tools in medical research. By exploring these issues, researchers can advance our understanding of how to harness the power of AI while minimizing its risks.	llama3.2:3b	ready	\N	0	2026-03-25 23:48:45.135094+05:30	{'Gap 1': 'Further investigation is needed into the potential biases introduced by AI-generated content in medical research. Researchers could explore using techniques such as data validation and peer review to mitigate these biases. Specific experiments could involve analyzing datasets generated by ChatGPT for signs of bias or error.', 'Gap 2': "The impact of ChatGPT on the scientific literacy of researchers is not well understood. Researchers could investigate how AI-generated content affects researchers' ability to critically evaluate evidence and design studies. This could involve designing experiments that compare the performance of human researchers with those using ChatGPT-generated content.", 'Gap 3': 'More research is needed into the potential applications of ChatGPT in medical research, such as automating literature reviews or data analysis. Researchers could explore developing new methods for integrating AI-generated content into existing research workflows.'}
dd163f38-8e5f-4a90-b2fb-edd372684711	ed00dbd8-c5fa-4433-8c7f-ae12affc4811	This study investigates the diagnostic accuracy of artificial intelligence (AI) models in identifying infantile hemangioma and recommending treatment options.	The authors used a retrospective multicenter diagnostic accuracy study design, analyzing data from 10 hospitals with 1,500 patients. They employed machine learning algorithms on a dataset containing clinical images, patient demographics, and medical histories. The AI models were trained using a combination of convolutional neural networks (CNNs) and recurrent neural networks (RNNs).	The study found that the AI model achieved an accuracy of 92.5% in diagnosing infantile hemangioma, compared to 85.2% for human pathologists. The model also showed a high sensitivity (95.1%) and specificity (90.3%) in recommending treatment options.	The study's retrospective design may introduce biases, and the sample size is limited to 10 hospitals. Additionally, the authors acknowledge that the dataset used may not be representative of all infantile hemangioma cases.	This study contributes to the development of AI-assisted diagnostic tools for pediatric dermatology, which can improve diagnosis accuracy and treatment outcomes. The findings also highlight the potential of machine learning algorithms in analyzing large datasets and identifying patterns that may not be apparent to human pathologists.	llama3.2:3b	failed	2026-03-26 23:18:47.642361+05:30	1	2026-03-26 23:17:35.509327+05:30	{'Investigating the effect of dataset quality on AI model performance: A researcher could explore how different data preprocessing techniques, feature engineering methods, or dataset sizes impact the accuracy of AI models in diagnosing infantile hemangioma. This study could involve analyzing multiple datasets with varying characteristics and evaluating their effects on model performance using metrics such as precision, recall, and F1-score. The results could inform the development of more robust and reliable AI-assisted diagnostic tools for pediatric dermatology. A potential experiment could involve collecting new data from a larger number of hospitals or clinics to create a more comprehensive dataset for training and testing AI models. Furthermore, exploring the use of transfer learning techniques to adapt pre-trained models to different datasets could provide insights into how to improve model generalizability and reduce overfitting. Finally, investigating the role of human pathologists in AI-assisted diagnosis could involve analyzing how human expertise can be integrated with AI outputs to improve overall diagnostic accuracy and treatment recommendations.': 'Investigating the clinical implications of AI-assisted diagnosis: A researcher could explore how AI models can be used to predict patient outcomes, identify high-risk cases, or monitor disease progression. This study could involve developing and validating machine learning algorithms that incorporate clinical data, imaging features, and patient demographics to predict infantile hemangioma recurrence or response to treatment. The results could inform the development of personalized treatment plans and improve patient care outcomes. A potential experiment could involve collecting longitudinal data on patients with infantile hemangioma who have received AI-assisted diagnosis and monitoring, analyzing how AI outputs correlate with clinical outcomes and identifying potential biomarkers for disease progression.', 'Investigating the ethics of AI-assisted diagnosis in pediatric dermatology: A researcher could explore the ethical implications of relying on AI models to diagnose and treat infantile hemangioma. This study could involve developing and validating machine learning algorithms that incorporate human values, such as patient privacy and informed consent, into decision-making processes. The results could inform the development of more transparent and accountable AI-assisted diagnostic tools for pediatric dermatology. A potential experiment could involve conducting surveys or interviews with patients, parents, and healthcare providers to understand their attitudes towards AI-assisted diagnosis and identifying potential areas for improvement in patient education and informed consent processes.': 'Investigating the role of human-AI collaboration in infantile hemangioma diagnosis: A researcher could explore how combining human expertise with AI outputs can improve diagnostic accuracy and treatment recommendations. This study could involve developing and validating machine learning algorithms that incorporate human feedback, clinical judgment, or expert knowledge into decision-making processes. The results could inform the development of more effective and efficient AI-assisted diagnostic tools for pediatric dermatology. A potential experiment could involve analyzing how human pathologists can be trained to work effectively with AI models, identifying potential areas for improvement in collaboration and communication between humans and machines.'}
ef310154-aaa6-4fa1-9f39-6fa60a2d42ca	33ae8721-e500-411a-99c1-c48c5544f61a	The paper addresses the gap in AI-driven cyberattack detection by providing a comprehensive review of recent advancements in Machine Learning, Deep Learning, Reinforcement Learning, Federated Learning, and emerging techniques such as generative AI, neuro-symbolic AI, swarm intelligence, lightweight AI, and quantum Computing.	The authors employ a literature review approach, analyzing the strengths and limitations of various AI-based cyberattack detection methods, including traditional signature-based IDS and anomaly-based detection. They also investigate the impact of computational resources, dataset representativeness, and evaluation metrics on AI model performance.	The paper highlights the performance of AI-driven detection methods on benchmark datasets such as KDDCUP, NSL-KDD, and MIMIC-III. The authors also discuss the importance of Explainable AI (XAI) in enhancing trust and transparency in AI-driven cyberattack detection.	The authors acknowledge limitations including computational costs, data quality issues, privacy concerns, and model interpretability challenges. They also note that traditional signature-based IDS are limited against novel attack patterns.	This paper matters as it advances the state-of-the-art in AI-driven cyberattack detection, providing a comprehensive review of recent advancements and highlighting the importance of Explainable AI (XAI) in enhancing trust and transparency. The paper enables researchers to develop robust, interpretable, and efficient solutions for securing complex digital environments.	llama3.2:3b	ready	2026-03-26 23:20:36.491122+05:30	1	2026-03-27 00:02:00.314497+05:30	The lack of up-to-date datasets for evaluating AI-driven cyberattack detection methods.\n\nThe limited exploration of hybrid quantum-classical models in AI-driven cyberattack detection.\n\nThe absence of a comprehensive evaluation framework for asynchronous Federated Learning protocols in AI-driven cyberattack detection.
429a7fb0-1050-4a0a-ba5c-85bbcc769c75	a2b397f1-57e0-4592-b623-53df14177f10	The authors aim to develop an artificial sensory transduction system inspired by biological magnetoreception in elasmobranchs for magnetic profile recognition and encryption.	The research employs moldable magnetic soft composites (MSCs) comprising magnetic particles in a zwitterionic polymer matrix, electromagnetic induction, and machine learning algorithms for decoding. The MSCs generate distinct transient induced electrical signals upon translocation through a receiving coil. Machine learning algorithms decode the static and dynamic information with ∼100% and 87.5% recognition accuracy, respectively.	The system achieves recognition strength of 3 bits and a large information-carrying capacity of 10. The authors recognize objects using electromagnetic induction to generate electrical intermediate signaling, coupled with machine learning for decoding.	Not specified in abstract	This paper advances the development of bioinspired artificial sensory transduction systems for various stimuli recognition and encryption applications. It contributes to the field of biomimetic materials and sensing technologies.	llama3.2:3b	ready	\N	0	2026-03-27 14:07:59.19563+05:30	The authors do not explore the potential limitations or constraints of using machine learning algorithms for decoding in noisy environments. A researcher could investigate how to improve the robustness of the system against noise and interference.\n\nNot specified in abstract\n\nThe paper does not discuss the long-term stability or durability of the moldable magnetic soft composites (MSCs) used in the system. A researcher could explore the degradation mechanisms and develop strategies for maintaining the performance of MSCs over time.
